// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Merge these fields into your real .devcontainer/devcontainer.json.
// This makes ANY contributor's own local SSH agent (with THEIR key, not
// yours) get forwarded into the container automatically -- as long as they
// have a normal ssh-agent running on their host with a key loaded, which is
// true for basically every dev setup (native ssh-agent, macOS Keychain
// agent, 1Password/Bitwarden SSH agent, WSL, etc).
//
// How it works: ${localEnv:SSH_AUTH_SOCK} is resolved on the HOST at
// container-start time, per person -- so teammate A forwards A's agent,
// teammate B forwards B's, you forward yours. Nobody's private key is ever
// copied into the image or the container filesystem.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
{
  "mounts": [
    "source=${localEnv:SSH_AUTH_SOCK},target=/ssh-agent,type=bind"
  ],
  "remoteEnv": {
    "SSH_AUTH_SOCK": "/ssh-agent"
  },
  "postCreateCommand": "bash .devcontainer/check-ssh-agent.sh"

  // Notes:
  // - VS Code's Dev Containers extension actually does SSH agent forwarding
  //   automatically even WITHOUT this config, on most setups. Adding it
  //   explicitly makes it work the same way for the standalone `devcontainer`
  //   CLI, docker compose, GitHub Codespaces, and any IDE that isn't VS Code
  //   -- i.e. it stops being "usually works" and becomes "always works."
  // - Host support: works out of the box for macOS, Linux, and WSL2 hosts
  //   (anywhere SSH_AUTH_SOCK is a real unix socket path). Native Windows
  //   without WSL2 uses a named pipe instead of a unix socket for its
  //   OpenSSH agent, which this bind-mount trick can't forward directly --
  //   those contributors should launch the devcontainer from a WSL2 window
  //   (which almost everyone building containers on Windows already does,
  //   since Docker Desktop's default backend IS WSL2).
}
